    'body'        => <<<'HTML'
<p>The best neighborhoods in Seattle to buy a home are the ones that match how you actually live: your commute, your budget, the kind of house you want, and whether you would rather walk to a brewery or a hiking trail. Seattle is a city of distinct neighborhoods, each with its own personality and housing stock, so the "best" choice for a young professional looks very different from the best choice for a growing family or a downsizer. Below we profile ten neighborhoods our clients ask about most, with an honest look at who each one suits.</p>

<h2>How to Think About the Best Neighborhoods in Seattle to Buy a Home</h2>
<p>Before you fall for a listing photo, get clear on a few priorities. They will narrow your search faster than anything else.</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Commute:</strong> Where do you work, and how will you get there? Proximity to Link light rail, major bus lines, or I-5 and SR 99 matters a lot.</li>
<li><strong>Housing type:</strong> Do you want a detached house with a yard, a low-maintenance townhome, or a condo?</li>
<li><strong>Daily life:</strong> Think about walkability, parks, schools, and the businesses you would use every week.</li>
<li><strong>Budget:</strong> Prices vary widely across the city. The same budget can buy a condo in one area and a house with a yard in another.</li>
</ul>
<p>With that in mind, here is our neighborhood-by-neighborhood rundown. You can dig deeper into each area in our full <a href="/neighborhoods/">Seattle neighborhood guide</a>.</p>

<h2>10 Seattle Neighborhoods Worth a Close Look</h2>

<h3>1. Ballard</h3>
<p><a href="/neighborhoods/ballard/">Ballard</a> blends its Nordic fishing-village heritage with one of the city's liveliest dining and brewery scenes. Historic Market Street, the Sunday farmers market, and the Ballard Locks give it a strong sense of place. Housing ranges from classic Craftsman bungalows to newer townhomes and condos near the commercial core. Ballard suits buyers who want walkable restaurants and nightlife without living downtown.</p>

<h3>2. Fremont</h3>
<p>Quirky and proud of it, <a href="/neighborhoods/fremont/">Fremont</a> calls itself the "Center of the Universe" and is home to the famous Fremont Troll under the Aurora Bridge. The Burke-Gilman Trail runs along the ship canal, making bike commuting easy. Expect a mix of older single-family homes on the hillsides and townhomes and condos closer to the water. Fremont fits creative types and tech workers who like character and a short hop to South Lake Union.</p>

<h3>3. Wallingford</h3>
<p><a href="/neighborhoods/wallingford/">Wallingford</a> is a classic Seattle neighborhood of tree-lined streets and well-kept Craftsman homes. N 45th Street offers local shops and restaurants, and Gas Works Park delivers one of the best skyline views in the city. It is a favorite for buyers who want a residential feel, older homes with charm, and a central location between Fremont, the University District, and Green Lake.</p>

<h3>4. Green Lake</h3>
<p>Life in <a href="/neighborhoods/green-lake/">Green Lake</a> revolves around the lake itself and its 2.8-mile loop, which fills with walkers, runners, and cyclists in every season. Homes range from early twentieth-century houses to condos and townhomes near the lake. Green Lake suits active buyers who want an outdoor amenity right outside the door and easy access to I-5 and nearby Roosevelt light rail.</p>

<h3>5. Greenwood</h3>
<p>Just north of Phinney Ridge and Woodland Park Zoo, <a href="/neighborhoods/greenwood/">Greenwood</a> has a friendly, neighborhood-main-street feel along Greenwood Avenue, with coffee shops, pubs, and independent stores. The housing stock leans toward bungalows and mid-century homes, with newer townhomes along the arterials. Greenwood often appeals to buyers who want a true neighborhood vibe and a bit more value than some of its southern neighbors.</p>

<h3>6. Queen Anne</h3>
<p>Perched above downtown, <a href="/neighborhoods/queen-anne/">Queen Anne</a> offers some of the most famous views in Seattle, especially from Kerry Park. Upper Queen Anne is known for stately Victorian-era and early twentieth-century homes, while Lower Queen Anne near Seattle Center has more apartments and condos. It suits buyers who value views, architecture, and a quick trip downtown.</p>

<h3>7. Capitol Hill</h3>
<p><a href="/neighborhoods/capitol-hill/">Capitol Hill</a> is the city's most energetic urban neighborhood, with the Pike/Pine corridor's restaurants and nightlife, Cal Anderson Park, and historic Volunteer Park. The light rail station makes car-free living realistic. Condos dominate near the core, while the north end has grand older homes on quieter streets. It is ideal for buyers who want density, culture, and transit.</p>

<h3>8. West Seattle</h3>
<p>Across the Duwamish, <a href="/neighborhoods/west-seattle/">West Seattle</a> feels like its own small city. Alki Beach, Lincoln Park, and the water taxi to downtown are big draws, and the junction areas offer shops and restaurants. Housing ranges from beach cottages and mid-century homes to newer townhomes. Commutes depend heavily on the West Seattle Bridge, so test yours. It is a great fit for buyers who want beach access and a laid-back pace.</p>

<h3>9. Magnolia</h3>
<p>Quiet and residential, <a href="/neighborhoods/magnolia/">Magnolia</a> is bordered by water on several sides and anchored by Discovery Park, the city's largest park. Homes tend to be single-family houses on larger lots, many with water or mountain views. Magnolia appeals to buyers who prioritize peace, nature, and space over nightlife.</p>

<h3>10. Columbia City</h3>
<p>In southeast Seattle, <a href="/neighborhoods/columbia-city/">Columbia City</a> centers on a historic business district along Rainier Avenue with diverse restaurants, a beloved farmers market, and a light rail station on the line to downtown and the airport. Housing includes Craftsman homes, newer townhomes, and condos. It suits buyers who want walkability, diversity, and transit access, often at a different price point than north end neighborhoods.</p>

