<h2>Neighborhoods to Keep on Your Radar</h2>
<p>If none of the ten above feels quite right, consider <a href="/neighborhoods/lake-city/">Lake City</a> in the northeast, which tends to be more affordable and is near Thornton Creek, or <a href="/neighborhoods/south-lake-union/">South Lake Union</a>, where condo living puts you steps from Lake Union Park, the streetcar, and major tech campuses. For neighborhood-level details like parks and community centers, Seattle Parks and Recreation at <a href="https://www.seattle.gov/parks">seattle.gov/parks</a> is a helpful resource.</p>

<h2>Tips for Touring Neighborhoods</h2>
<ol>
<li>Visit on a weekday evening and a weekend morning to see the full rhythm of the area.</li>
<li>Drive or ride your real commute during rush hour at least once.</li>
<li>Walk a few blocks in every direction from a home you like. Streets can change quickly.</li>
<li>Ask about future development nearby, especially near transit, where new construction is common.</li>
</ol>

